\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The synthesis methodology using the Cadence Genus tool follows a structured
 and constraint-driven approach to transform RTL into a gate-level netlist
 optimized for area, power, and timing.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The process begins with the 
\series bold
preparation stage
\series default
, where all RTL design files, technology libraries, and constraint files
 (typically in SDC format) are gathered and organized.
 This is followed by the 
\series bold
elaboration
\series default
 and analysis phase, during which Genus reads and elaborates the RTL to
 create an internal design representation and checks for structural correctness,
 connectivity, and design rule violations.
 After elaboration, 
\series bold
user-defined constraints are applied
\series default
 to define clocking, input/output delays, and timing exceptions.
 These constraints guide the timing engine to establish the setup and hold
 relationships within the design.
 Once constraints are in place, the synthesis engine 
\series bold
maps the RTL logic to standard cell gates
\series default
 using the provided technology library.
 Genus performs a series of logic optimizations, such as retiming, resource
 sharing, and constant propagation, to meet the design goals for area, power,
 and performance.
 In cases where aggressive optimization is needed, multi-pass synthesis
 strategies may be employed to iteratively improve results.
 After synthesis, the tool generates a 
\series bold
gate-level netlist
\series default
 along with detailed 
\series bold
timing, area, and power reports
\series default
.
 These reports help identify critical timing paths, violations, and design
 bottlenecks.
 The final stage involves verifying the correctness of the synthesized netlist,
 checking for issues such as latch inference, combinational loops, or unused
 logic.
 Once verified, the synthesized netlist and associated reports are handed
 off to the physical design team for place and route using tools like Cadence
 Innovus.
 This methodology ensures a clean, optimized, and implementation-ready netlist
 that aligns with the design specifications and physical constraints of
 the target technology.
\end_layout

