\begin_layout Subsection
Zero Delay Simulations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the initial phases of Gate-Level Simulation (GLS), while the design is
 still undergoing timing closure, zero-delay simulations are often used
 to validate functional correctness.
 These simulations run significantly faster than those with full timing.
 Zero-delay mode can be activated using the 
\series bold
\emph on
-NOSpecify
\series default
\emph default
 option.
 However, since all signal transitions occur at the same simulation time,
 there's a higher risk of introducing race conditions.
 To mitigate this, some delay may be added to gates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Cadence provides multiple delay modes for gate-level simulation, such as
 
\emph on
delay_mode_path
\emph default
, 
\emph on
delay_mode_distributed
\emph default
, 
\emph on
delay_mode_unit
\emph default
, and 
\emph on
delay_mode_zero
\emph default
.
 Replacing detailed delay modes like path or distributed with global delays
 like zero or unit can substantially speed up simulation.
 These simplified delay modes are especially useful during the debugging
 phase, when verifying design functionality takes precedence over timing
 accuracy.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Identifying zero delay loops
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Xcelium simulator includes a built-in feature that identifies potential
 zero-delay combinational loops and generates a warning when such loops
 are found.
 Detecting these loops is crucial, as they can cause glitches that may propagate
 through the design.
 This feature can be activated for Verilog simulations by using the 
\series bold
\emph on
-GAteloopwarn
\series default
\emph default
 option on the command line.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Handling Zero-delay race conditions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Race conditions in zero-delay mode can arise because all gates have zero
 propagation delay by default.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Float figure
placement H
wide false
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename /home/rclab/Documents/ASIC Design/Zero_delay_loops.pdf
	scale 80

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Zero 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "fig:Zero-delay-loops"

\end_inset

delay loops.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As shown in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Zero-delay-loops"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

, such issues can occur when there is combinational logic in the clock path
 during zero-delay simulation.
 In this scenario, a race may happen, causing the data at FF2 to be captured
 at the same clock edge as it exits FF1.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To resolve this, a unit delay (e.g., #1) can be introduced at the output of
 FF1 to ensure that its data is delayed and thus gets latched into FF2 on
 the following clock edge.
 One way to implement this is by inserting a buffer with a unit delay, as
 shown in the following example:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
buf #1 buf _ prim _ delay1 (D2 _ FF2, Data _ OUT _ FF1); //Add buffer with
 unit delay.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
